   is `bg-[linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(10,22,48,N),rgba(2,7,20,N))]` with a
   `text-white` heading inside it, tuned only ever against a dark page. Editing
   every one of those files individually is a much larger change than this
   pass — these three rules retarget the pattern itself, wherever it appears,
   for light mode only. Dark mode does not match any of these selectors and is
   completely unaffected.

   The attribute selector matches the literal characters Tailwind puts in the
   `class` DOM attribute, which is unescaped there even though the generated
   CSS selector for the same class has to escape the brackets and commas — so
   `[class*="rgba(10,22,48"]` finds it without needing to know the exact
   opacity any given instance used. */
html[data-theme="light"] [class*="rgba(10,22,48"]:not(.nx-dark-island):not(.nx-dark-island *) {
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255,255,255,.92), rgba(246,249,253,.98)) !important;
  border-color: var(--color-rim);
}
html[data-theme="light"] [class*="rgba(6,14,36"]:not(.nx-dark-island):not(.nx-dark-island *) {
  background: rgba(255,255,255,.85) !important;
}
html[data-theme="light"] [class*="rgba(4,11,30"]:not(.nx-dark-island):not(.nx-dark-island *) {
  background: #ffffff !important;
  border-color: var(--color-rim) !important;
}
/* A `text-white` heading is only ever meant as "the readable ink colour" in
   these components — never a colour chosen to sit on a saturated brand
   surface, which is what the VPS panel's own remaining `text-white` spots are
   (and why those were fixed by hand instead of matched here). Scoped to
   descendants of the two card patterns above, so nothing outside them moves. */
html[data-theme="light"] [class*="rgba(10,22,48"]:not(.nx-dark-island) [class~="text-white"]:not(.nx-dark-island *),
html[data-theme="light"] [class*="rgba(6,14,36"]:not(.nx-dark-island) [class~="text-white"]:not(.nx-dark-island *),
html[data-theme="light"] [class*="rgba(4,11,30"][class~="text-white"]:not(.nx-dark-island):not(.nx-dark-island *) {
  color: var(--color-tx) !important;
}

/* The exact opposite bug, same root cause: a `text-white` sitting on a
   hard-coded purple/blue gradient FILL (the VPS plan-selection step's
   "Selected" button, its "Most popular" ribbon, the storage quick-picks, the
   hosting-plan ribbon) wants literal white always — the fill never changes
   with the theme, only the token does. In dark mode that is invisible (both
   are `#fff`), but in light mode `--color-white` is deliberately flipped to
   near-black ink for ordinary headings, and this button caught that flip too:
   black text on a purple button. A handful of these spots already dodge it by
   writing `text-[#fff]` instead of `text-white` (see OrderBits.tsx); this one
   catches the ones that do not, wherever the same literal gradient is used,
   without having to hunt down every call site by hand. */
html[data-theme="light"] [class*="bg-[linear-gradient(100deg,#1a55cf,#6f2ee0)]"][class~="text-white"] {
65:  --color-cyan: #14c5e4;
359:  --color-cyan: #0a7790;
653:  --color-cyan: #14c5e4;
834:  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-cyan) 45%, transparent);
835:  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-cyan) 12%, var(--color-well));

